# Master Build Prompt — Generic Exception Closure & Validation System

Use this prompt as-is with an AI coding assistant (Claude Code, Cursor, etc.)
to generate the complete system in one pass, or to extend it. It is
self-contained — no other context needed.

---

## Objective

Build a configuration-driven exception-closure engine that replaces
per-product, per-rule-ID hardcoded validators. A rule is ONE object that
may run business logic directly against an exception record, or first run
an ordered pipeline of pre-steps (enrich from an external source, or
pivot/aggregate the dataset itself) — each conditionally applied — before
the business logic runs. When satisfied, the rule stamps a mandatory
reason code and rendered commentary. A single generic validator function
applies whatever rules are currently active, for ANY product, with zero
product- or rule-ID-specific code, and automatically reflects a rule
amendment on the very next call — no restart, no redeploy.

---

## Part 1 — Rule schema (`rule_schema_v2.py`, Pydantic v2)

- `PRODUCT_FIELDS: Dict[str, Dict[str, str]]` — per-product field name →
  type (`numeric | categorical | identifier`). Seed with `cash_bonds`, `fx`,
  `repo`; adding a product later means adding one entry here, nothing else.
- `Operator` enum: `gt, gte, lt, lte, between` (numeric) and
  `eq, ne, in, not_in` (categorical/identifier).
- `RuleStatus`: `draft, pending, active, disabled, deprecated`.
- `Severity`: `low, medium, high, critical`.
- `Condition{field, operator, value}` and recursive
  `CompositeCondition{logic: AND|OR, conditions: [Condition|CompositeCondition]}`.
- `DeriveSpec{output_field, op, a, b}` — op restricted to
  `abs_diff, diff, sum, ratio, pct_diff`. Never eval() user input.
- `EnrichStep{step_id, apply_when: Optional[condition], source_name,
  join_on, fields: {source_col: output_field}, field_types, derive[]}`.
- `PivotStep{step_id, apply_when: Optional[condition], group_by[],
  agg_function (sum|count|avg|min|max), agg_field, output_field, derive[]}`.
- `Rule{rule_id, name, product, version, status, severity, owner, mode
  (direct|enriched), pre_steps: [EnrichStep|PivotStep], priority: int
  (lower runs first), condition, reason_code_id: str (mandatory,
  non-blank), commentary: str (mandatory, non-blank, supports {field}
  placeholders), created_at, updated_at, notes}`.
- Model validator: `mode="direct"` forbids `pre_steps`; `mode="enriched"`
  requires at least one; `step_id` unique within a rule.

## Part 2 — Field resolution & validation (`field_resolver.py`)

The core mechanism. Fields become available progressively:
`stage 0 = PRODUCT_FIELDS[product]`; `after pre_step[i] = stage i +
pre_step[i]'s declared outputs`. A step's `apply_when` may only reference
fields available BEFORE that step (it cannot gate on its own output). The
business `condition` may reference base fields plus ALL pre-step outputs.

- `SOURCE_SCHEMAS: Dict[str, Dict[str, str]]` — declared columns/types per
  external source (e.g. `issuer_credit_events`, `manual_override_log`,
  `counterparty_master`, `fx_benchmark_rates`).
- `fields_before_step(product, pre_steps, index) -> {field: type}`.
- `step_outputs(step) -> {field: type}` — an enrich step's outputs come
  from its `fields` mapping (typed via `field_types` or the source
  schema); a pivot step's output is always numeric; derive outputs are
  always numeric.
- `validate_condition(cond, available_fields, where) -> [errors]` —
  recursive; checks field exists at this stage, operator matches type,
  `between`/`in`/`not_in` shape correctness.
- `validate_rule(rule_dict) -> (Rule|None, [errors])` — the single
  validation entry point: schema validation, then per-step gate/source/
  join/aggregate checks against the fields available AT THAT STAGE, then
  the business condition against ALL available fields. Collect every
  error; never stop at the first one.

## Part 3 — Pipeline engine (`pipeline_engine.py`)

- `eval_condition(cond, record) -> bool` — recursive AND/OR evaluation;
  missing field or type-coercion failure returns `False`, never raises.
- `run_rule(rule, exceptions_df, sources, dataset_override=None) -> [{record, trail, decision}]`:
  precompute pivot aggregates ONCE per rule call (group_by + agg over the
  whole dataset), then per record: run each pre_step in declared order
  (skip — not error — on `apply_when` false or on no source/aggregate
  match, recording why in the trail), apply whitelisted `derive` ops,
  then evaluate the business condition. Satisfied → `{outcome: closed,
  reason_code_id, commentary: rendered with {field} substitution from the
  prepared record}`. Not satisfied → `{outcome: open}`.

## Part 4 — Persistence (`rule_store_v2.py`)

`RuleStore(base_dir)`:
- One YAML file per rule at `rules/<rule_id>.yml`; history backups at
  `rules/_history/<rule_id>_v<timestamp>.yml`; append-only
  `audit_log.jsonl` (timestamp, action, rule_id, actor, detail).
- Atomic writes: temp file in the same directory + `rename`.
- `validate(rule_dict) -> Rule` — calls `field_resolver.validate_rule()`,
  raises `RuleValidationError` with the full readable error list on
  failure.
- `create`, `update` (bumps version; **does not reset status** — amending
  an active rule keeps it active), `get`, `list(product=, status=)`.
- Lifecycle: `promote` (draft/pending/disabled → active), `disable`
  (active → disabled), `deprecate` (any non-deprecated → deprecated,
  terminal). Illegal transitions raise with the current status and the
  legal source states named.
- `fingerprint(product) -> str` — sorted `"rule_id:version"` pairs for
  currently-ACTIVE rules of that product. Changes if and only if the
  active rule set for that product changed (promote/disable/deprecate/
  amend-while-active). This is what makes "no restart needed" possible.

## Part 5 — Runtime loading (`rule_registry.py`)

`RuleRegistry(store)`:
- `get_active_rules(product) -> [Rule]`, sorted by `priority`. Internally:
  compare `store.fingerprint(product)` to the cached value for that
  product; if different, reload from the store and update the cache;
  otherwise return the cached list. Expose a `reload_count` counter for
  observability/testing.
- This is the ONLY place that decides whether to hit the store again — no
  other component should cache rules itself.

## Part 6 — Generic validator (`generic_validator.py`)

`validate_exceptions(product, exceptions_df, sources, registry) ->
DataFrame with columns added: status (CLEARED|ALERTED), matched_rule,
reason_code_id, commentary`.

Implementation constraint (verify by inspecting the function's source): it
must contain **no product name and no rule_id literal** anywhere in its
executable body — only generic field/condition logic via
`registry.get_active_rules(product)` and `pipeline_engine.run_rule()`.
Rules evaluate in priority order; first one whose condition is satisfied
for a given record wins; a record no rule closes is ALERTED. This single
function is the ONLY validator needed for every product, present and
future.

## Part 7 — UI (single HTML file, no build step, no framework)

Two tabs:

**Rule Console** — left rail: product + status filters, rule list with
status pill (draft/active/disabled/deprecated color-coded), version,
product/mode tags. Right pane, rule editor:
- Rule ID (immutable after create), name, product (immutable after
  create), owner, severity, priority.
- Mode toggle: Direct / Enriched-pivoted. Enriched reveals a **pre-step
  builder**: ordered step cards, each showing an "Apply when" gate
  (dropdown of fields available BEFORE that step; "+ Add condition" if
  none set, meaning always-run), then either an enrich config (source
  dropdown, join-on dropdown scoped to available fields, source-column →
  output-field mapping rows) or a pivot config (multi-select group_by,
  agg function + field, output field name), then a derived-fields section
  (output name, op, operand A, operand B — operand dropdowns scoped to
  fields available at that point). Each step card shows "Adds to the
  record: <list>".
- Business logic section: condition builder whose field dropdown is
  scoped to base fields + ALL pre-step outputs (stage-aware, matching
  `field_resolver`).
- Closure section: reason code ID input, commentary input with a hint
  listing available `{field}` placeholders.
- Action bar: **Run test (dry run)** (shows match table + per-record step
  trail), **Save as draft / Save changes**, **Promote to active** (shown
  only when legal), **Disable** (only from active), **Deprecate** (any
  non-deprecated).
- Validation errors render in a distinct panel, verbatim, before anything
  saves.

**Exception Analysis** — product dropdown, uploaded-dataset dropdown
(simulate 1-2 named sample datasets per product), "Run validation"
button. A status strip shows which active rules were loaded for the
selected product (as chips) and a running registry-reload counter, so the
caching/reload behavior is visible, not just correct. Results render as a
table: trade ref, CLEARED/ALERTED pill, matched rule, reason code,
commentary. Switching tabs preserves state; creating/amending/promoting a
rule in the Rule Console tab and returning to re-run validation must show
the updated outcome with no page reload.

Design language: internal ops console, not consumer SaaS — flat surfaces,
hairline borders, monospace for IDs/field names/SQL-like content,
sans-serif for labels, status colors (draft=grey, pending=amber,
active=green, disabled=grey, deprecated=red).

