## Testing requirements — do not skip any of these

1. **Schema/resolver tests** (pure Python, no browser): unknown field
   rejected at the correct pipeline stage; operator/type mismatch
   rejected; a step's `apply_when` referencing its own output rejected; a
   business condition referencing a field no step produces rejected;
   direct mode with pre_steps rejected; enriched mode with zero pre_steps
   rejected; missing/blank `reason_code_id` or `commentary` rejected;
   product-field scoping enforced (a cash_bonds field rejected on an fx
   rule); a well-formed direct rule, enriched rule (with a conditional
   enrich step + derive), and pivot rule all validate and produce correct
   dry-run match counts against a small hand-built sample dataset,
   cross-checked by manually filtering the same data.
2. **Store/registry/validator tests**: full lifecycle (create → update →
   promote → disable → re-promote → deprecate) with illegal transitions
   rejected; `fingerprint()` changes if and only if the active set
   changed; amending an ALREADY-ACTIVE rule does not reset its status and
   is picked up on the immediate next `get_active_rules()` call with no
   explicit reload call; disabling a rule removes it from the very next
   call; two active rules for one product resolved by `priority`; a
   second product's dataset never matches the first product's rules;
   the generic validator's source code contains no product/rule_id
   literals (assert by inspecting `inspect.getsource()`), and the exact
   same function correctly validates all three seeded products.
3. **Real headless-browser UI tests** — required, not optional, and must
   be a genuine browser, not a DOM-less reimplementation:
   - If `puppeteer`'s default Chrome download is blocked by network
     policy (common in sandboxed/corporate environments — downloads from
     `storage.googleapis.com`), do NOT fall back to logic-only testing.
     Instead use `puppeteer-core` + `@sparticuz/chromium`, which ships a
     real Chromium binary INSIDE the npm package tarball (from
     `registry.npmjs.org`), extractable and launchable with zero external
     download. Install with `PUPPETEER_SKIP_DOWNLOAD=true npm install
     puppeteer-core @sparticuz/chromium`; get the binary path via
     `require('@sparticuz/chromium').default.executablePath()`.
   - Click through: create a rule with an invalid pre-step config, verify
     the exact validation panel error text; fix it; run a dry-run and
     verify the rendered match table; save; walk the full lifecycle
     through the actual buttons (not by calling JS functions directly)
     checking status text after each click, watching for CSS
     `text-transform` quirks (`innerText` reflects rendered case, not
     source case — assert case-insensitively or you'll get false
     failures); build a brand-new enriched rule end-to-end in the UI
     (typed fields, not pre-seeded data) and confirm its dry-run output;
     switch to Exception Analysis, run validation, then go amend the
     SAME active rule's threshold with no promote step, return, re-run
     on the same dataset with no page reload, and confirm the outcome
     visibly changes for at least one record.
   - When a browser test fails, verify which side the bug is on before
     "fixing": a wrong CSS selector picking the wrong element (e.g. two
     `<select>` elements on a page — confirm which one a broad selector
     actually matched) is a common false-positive; only patch the
     application if the failure reproduces with a maximally-specific
     selector too.

